steps.body:
Sometimes you need to know where each level ends, such as counting the minutes while something spreads. Process the queue in rounds: save `len(q)`, dequeue exactly that many cells, then count one round.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	q := [][2]int{{0, 0}, {2, 1}}
	minutes := 0
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("minutes:", minutes)
}
```

```go
for len(q) > 0 {
	size := len(q)
	for i := 0; i < size; i++ {
		p := q[0]
		q = q[1:]
		_ = p // queue p's neighbors for the next round
	}
	minutes++
}
```

Save the size first. The inner loop dequeues and appends while it runs, so `len(q)` keeps changing and `i < len(q)` would cut this level short or mix in the next one.

When the spread starts from several places at once, put every source in the queue before the loop. That is multi-source BFS: all sources are level 0, and each cell is reached by its nearest source.

Mark a cell by changing it in the grid, so it is never queued twice. Strings are immutable, so store the rows as `[]byte(line)`.

